  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes Instabridge Different</h2>
  <p>
    Instabridge didn't start as an eSIM company. It's a WiFi-sharing app first: its own site advertises access to over <strong>20 million free WiFi hotspots worldwide</strong>, a community-built database of hotspot passwords that predates its eSIM product. The eSIM plans are a bolt-on to that, sold as a fallback for when free WiFi isn't around or isn't fast enough. That's a genuinely different starting point from a provider like <a href="/airalo/">Airalo</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a>, which only ever sold data.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice that means the free WiFi and the paid eSIM aren't really separate products, they're two ways of solving the same problem inside one app. Whether that's worth anything to you depends entirely on whether free hotspot coverage is decent where you're headed: a community-submitted database is only as good as the community submitting to it, so it's stronger in dense, well-travelled cities than in rural areas or newer destinations.
  </p>

  <!-- Codes -->
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
